    <div class="blog-post">
      <div class="blog-body">
        <div class="blog-warning">
          <strong>Smell gas, or a carbon monoxide alarm going off?</strong>
          <p>That is not a noise problem. Turn the boiler off if you can, open the windows and get everyone outside. Call the National Gas Emergency line on <a href="tel:0800111999">0800 111 999</a>. Then call us once it is safe.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>The Short Answer</h2>
        <p>Most boiler noises come from one of four things: air in the system, sludge or limescale, low or high water pressure, or a part wearing out. The noise itself is a good clue to which one it is.</p>

        <div class="blog-stat">
          <div class="stat-num">£120</div>
          <div class="stat-label">Our first hour for a boiler repair, weekdays 9am to 4pm, including finding the fault. No call-out fee.</div>
        </div>
        <CallStrip inline title="Boiler making a strange noise?" text="Tell us what it sounds like. Our Gas Safe engineers come out 24/7." />

        <h2>Banging or Rumbling, Like a Kettle</h2>
        <p>This is often called kettling. Sludge or limescale builds up on the heat exchanger inside the boiler. Water gets trapped and overheats in small pockets, then boils with a bang or a rumble.</p>
        <p>Kettling is more common in older systems, and where the system has never been cleaned. Signs that go with it:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Radiators cold at the bottom</li>
          <li>Dark or black water when you bleed a radiator</li>
          <li>The heating takes longer to warm up than it used to</li>
        </ul>
        <p>A kettling boiler works harder and wears out sooner. The usual fix is to clean the system with a <a href="/power-flushing-liverpool">power flush</a> and fit a magnetic filter. If the heat exchanger is badly scaled, it may need more work. We tell you the price before we start.</p>

        <h2>Whistling</h2>
        <p>A whistle is usually a milder version of kettling, from scale or sludge restricting flow. It can also come from low pressure, or air trapped in the system.</p>
        <p>Check the pressure gauge first. It should read 1 to 1.5 bar when the heating is off. If it is lower, <a href="/boiler-losing-pressure-liverpool">top up the pressure</a> and listen again.</p>

        <h2>Gurgling</h2>
        <p>Gurgling usually means air in the system, or low pressure. Bleed the radiators and check the pressure. See our guide to <a href="/blog/radiator-cold-at-top-or-bottom-liverpool">bleeding radiators</a>.</p>
        <div class="blog-callout">
          <strong>Gurgling in freezing weather, then the boiler stops?</strong>
          <p>That is very likely a frozen condensate pipe. You can often fix it yourself in 15 minutes with warm water. See <a href="/frozen-condensate-pipe-liverpool">how to thaw a frozen condensate pipe</a>.</p>
        </div>

        <h2>Humming or Vibrating</h2>
        <p>A loud hum often comes from the pump. It may be set too high, or starting to wear out. It can also be a loose pipe or a boiler that is not fixed firmly to the wall.</p>
        <p>Pumps and fans are common parts to replace on an older boiler. An engineer can tell which one it is in a short visit.</p>

        <h2>Clicking, and the Boiler Will Not Light</h2>
        <p>A few clicks when the boiler starts is normal. That is the ignition making a spark. Clicking over and over, with no flame and then a fault code, means the boiler is trying to light and failing.</p>
        <p>Check the gas is on by trying your gas hob. If it lights, reset the boiler once. If it locks out again, call us. Look up the code on our <a href="/boiler-repair-liverpool#error-codes">boiler error codes</a> list.</p>

        <h2>Dripping, Hissing or Water Coming From a Pipe Outside</h2>
        <p>If water drips from the copper pipe outside near the boiler, the pressure may be too high. The boiler is letting water out through its safety valve. This often happens when the pressure gauge goes above 2.5 to 3 bar.</p>
        <p>Check the filling loop under the boiler is fully closed. If the pressure keeps climbing, the expansion vessel or filling valve may need looking at. This needs a Gas Safe engineer.</p>

        <h2>What You Can Safely Check Yourself</h2>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>The pressure gauge.</strong> 1 to 1.5 bar when cold. Top up if it is low.</li>
          <li><strong>Bleed the radiators</strong> if you hear gurgling or they are cold at the top.</li>
          <li><strong>The condensate pipe</strong> outside, if it is freezing.</li>
          <li><strong>Reset the boiler once</strong> if it shows a fault code.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>Do not take the boiler casing off. By law, only a Gas Safe registered engineer can work on the gas parts of a boiler.</p>

