        <CallStrip inline title="Want us to take a look?" text="Call for advice or a price. Gas Safe registered, no call-out fee." />

        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
        <div class="pf-faq">
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">Is a noisy boiler dangerous?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Usually not on its own. Most noises come from air, sludge or pressure. But a noisy boiler is working harder than it should, and the fault can get worse. If you smell gas, see soot or a yellow flame, or your CO alarm goes off, call 0800 111 999 straight away.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">What is boiler kettling?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>A banging or rumbling noise like a kettle boiling. Sludge or limescale on the heat exchanger traps water, which overheats and boils. Cleaning the system with a power flush, then fitting a magnetic filter, is the usual fix.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">Why is my boiler gurgling?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>Usually air in the system or low pressure. Bleed the radiators and top up the pressure to 1 to 1.5 bar. In freezing weather, a gurgle followed by a fault code is often a frozen condensate pipe.</p></div></details>
          <details class="pf-faq-item"><summary><span class="pf-faq-q">Can a boiler service stop the noise?</span></summary><div class="pf-faq-body"><p>A service cleans and checks the boiler, and can catch a part that is wearing out. But it does not clean sludge out of the radiators and pipes. If sludge is the cause, the system needs a power flush.</p></div></details>
        </div>
      </div>

